# The self-test through the driver (docs/circuits.md, "Configuration and the self-test"): fminus --self-test runs it on
# a netlist that leaves its configuration open (Inputs/clipper-pot.cir), prints each candidate it tried, cheapest first,
# with its verdict, then a line of directives to paste, and generates the module with the answer, whose header names
# the self-test and its key. The answer and the notes are the same on any number of threads.
# RUN: fminus %S/Inputs/clipper-pot.cir --self-test --threads 1 --emit fm -o %t.1.fm 2> %t.1.notes
# RUN: FileCheck %s --check-prefix=NOTES --input-file %t.1.notes
# RUN: FileCheck %s --check-prefix=MODULE --input-file %t.1.fm
# RUN: fminus %S/Inputs/clipper-pot.cir --self-test --threads 4 --emit fm -o %t.4.fm 2> %t.4.notes
# RUN: diff %t.1.notes %t.4.notes
# RUN: diff %t.1.fm %t.4.fm

# NOTES:      clipper-pot.cir: note: self-test 087ad67714f6249a: .oversample, .adaa, .solver, .method open; 3 points x 17 signals = 51 units, L = 4.5 V (.target level); 16 candidates, cheapest first
# NOTES-NEXT: clipper-pot.cir: note: self-test candidate 1: 1x, omega(1), 152 model cycles: {{.*}}: fails (T2 {{.*}})
# NOTES-NEXT: clipper-pot.cir: note: self-test candidate 2: 1x, ADAA, omega(1), 209 model cycles: {{.*}}: fails
# NOTES:      clipper-pot.cir: note: self-test candidate 9: 4x, omega(1), 680 model cycles: {{.*}}: fails (T1 {{.*}})
# NOTES-NEXT: clipper-pot.cir: note: self-test candidate 10: 4x, ADAA, omega(1), 908 model cycles: T1 {{.*}} dB, T2 {{.*}} dB, T3 {{.*}} dB: passes
# NOTES-NEXT: clipper-pot.cir: note: self-test: the answer is candidate 10, 4x, ADAA, omega(1): the first that meets T1-T4
# NOTES-NEXT: clipper-pot.cir: note: the self-test chose .oversample 4, .adaa on, .solver omega(1)
# NOTES-NOT:  {{.}}

# MODULE:      // PotClipper: generated from clipper-pot.cir by fminus::circuit (Phase 1). Do not edit: edit clipper-pot.cir.
# MODULE:      //   configuration: .oversample 4, .adaa on, .solver omega(1), .method tr (the self-test 087ad67714f6249a); iir blocks=auto (default)
# MODULE:      void b1_set(float drive)

# Without --self-test the same netlist plays its provisional configuration; --notes prints the plan, the provisional
# note first, and with each block that has pots, the form of its b<N>_set (the Woodbury form only where it does less).
# RUN: fminus %S/Inputs/clipper-pot.cir --notes --emit fm -o %t.p.fm 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=PLAN --match-full-lines
# PLAN:      {{.*}}clipper-pot.cir: note: the self-test chooses this circuit's configuration; provisional: 8x IIR, omega(2)
# PLAN-NEXT: {{.*}}clipper-pot.cir: note: block 1 (R1, C1, D1, D2): 1 state, port D1|D2, at 8 rate, omega3 + 2 Newton steps
# PLAN-NEXT: {{.*}}clipper-pot.cir: note: block 1's b1_set: all in set, 8 operations (the Woodbury form's would be 8, and 0 in init)
# PLAN-NEXT: {{.*}}clipper-pot.cir: note: latency: about 3.8 host samples at 1 kHz (IIR halfbands)

# A netlist that names its whole configuration has nothing to choose; its key (pinned here: it moves only with the
# netlist's lines, the Options or kSelfTestVersion) is still printed.
# RUN: fminus %S/../../../examples/circuits/diodetone.cir --self-test --emit fm -o %t.dt.fm 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=PINNED
# PINNED: diodetone.cir: note: self-test 874a81249f5a77a4: the netlist names its whole configuration (or has no port): nothing to choose

# An #included netlist is tested too, and its module generated with the answer.
# RUN: fminus %S/Inputs/play-pot.fm -I %S/Inputs --self-test --run peak 4.5 2> %t.inc.notes | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=PEAK
# RUN: FileCheck %s --check-prefix=INCLUDED --input-file %t.inc.notes
# INCLUDED: clipper-pot.cir: note: the self-test chose .oversample 4, .adaa on, .solver omega(1)
# PEAK: 0.{{[0-9]+$}}
